# Devour one corpse of NEGATIVE challenge rating and one of positive challenge
# rating, and photograph the game's own experience-point counter either side of
# each meal. This is the session for bd inc-i9p.
#
#   tools/headless.sh tools/keys/devour-negative-cr.keys 4
#
# Read the result with tools/check_devour_negative_cr.sh, which is what asserts
# on the numbers. This file only produces the screens.
#
# WHAT THE BEAD CLAIMED. Creature::DevourMonster (src/Skills.cpp) ends with
#
#     i = CR * 50;
#     for (j = ChallengeRating(); j >= CR; j--) i /= 2;
#     GainXP(i);
#
# i and j are int16 and Character::GainXP takes a uint32, so a NEGATIVE i would
# be converted to a huge unsigned number and would subtract experience instead
# of adding it. A myconid is written "CR: -2" in lib/mon2.irh, so the bead read
# that as an experience LOSS of six points for a 1st-level devourer.
#
# WHAT THIS SESSION FOUND. It does not happen, because CR never reaches this
# arithmetic as a negative number. Creature::ChallengeRating (src/Creature.cpp)
# is declared "int16 ChallengeRating(bool allow_neg = false)" and its last line
# is "return max(0,CR)". DevourMonster calls it with no argument, so a CR -2
# myconid arrives here as ZERO and the award is 0 * 50 = 0.
#
# THE ORACLE IS THE STATUS SIDEBAR, which the game writes as "<n>/<next> XP"
# (src/Term.cpp:369). Four photographs, and the comparison between them is the
# whole test:
#
#   xp-before-myconid   after the myconid is killed, before it is eaten
#   xp-after-myconid    after it is eaten -- MUST NOT be lower
#   xp-before-tabaxi    after the tabaxi is killed, before it is eaten
#   xp-after-tabaxi     after it is eaten -- MUST be higher
#
# THE TABAXI IS THE CONTROL AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. Without it, a session in
# which DevourMonster returned early -- or in which the character had no
# Devouring ability at all -- would show an unchanged number and would be read
# as proof that nothing was subtracted. The tabaxi is written "CR: 2"
# (lib/mon2.irh), the devourer stands at character level 1, so the halving loop
# never runs and the award is the full 2 * 50 = 100 points. A run that does not
# see that award has not exercised the code this bead is about.
#
# WHY THESE TWO MONSTERS. The myconid is the bead's own worked example and is
# the lowest-CR creature the summon menu offers in a group that can be named.
# The tabaxi is the cheapest CR 2 creature that leaves an ordinary corpse: the
# two githyanki-family monsters at the same CR carry CA_PHASE, which puts a
# PHASED stati on the corpse (src/Fight.cpp) and hides it from a devourer
# standing on the material plane.
#
# WIZARD MODE SUPPLIES BOTH, because no scripted walk can be relied on to find
# a named creature. Freeze Monsters is switched on first (the eleventh row of
# the Wizard Mode Switches list) so that neither summoned creature acts: the
# myconid's spore attack is 3d6 AD_STUN, and a stunned character stops
# answering the script.
#
# THE THREE IDIOMS THAT LOOK ODD, and all three are the game, not the script.
#
# 1. The first blow at each creature raises "Confirm attack the <name>?"
#    (src/Player.cpp), because a freshly summoned creature is not yet hostile
#    to the player's party. The bare RIGHT before each @until is the keystroke
#    that raises that question and the @while is what answers it; afterwards
#    the creature is hostile and the question is not asked again.
# 2. @until "* <name>" RIGHT keeps swinging until the creature's glyph in the
#    Things in View list turns from a creature into an item pile, which is how
#    the script knows it is dead without counting blows.
# 3. Stepping onto the corpse opens a "You see here:" panel that swallows the
#    next keystroke, and the eat command is lowercase 'e' -- StandardKeySet
#    lists KY_CMD_EAT with no modifier, and TokenToKey (src/Wposix.cpp) sets
#    SHIFT for an uppercase letter, so a capital E reaches nothing at all.
#
# The "You finish eating ..." sentence is read off the message log rather than
# off the map, because the map's message window holds two lines and the
# alignment and disease messages that a corpse produces push that sentence off
# it within the same turn.

@include chargen.keys

# Freeze Monsters, the eleventh row of the switches list.
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
DOWN*10
SPACE
ESC

# --- the CR -2 corpse: a myconid ---------------------------------------------
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Aberration"
@choose "myconid"
RIGHT
ENTER

RIGHT
@while "Confirm attack" y
@until "* myconid" RIGHT
@dump:xp-before-myconid

RIGHT
@while "You see here" ESC
e
@choose "(ground) myconid corpse"
@while "Stop eating" n
@dump:xp-after-myconid
v
@dump:log-myconid
@while "Messages" ESC

# --- the CR 2 control: a tabaxi ----------------------------------------------
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Cats"
@choose "tabaxi"
RIGHT
ENTER

RIGHT
@while "Confirm attack" y
@until "* tabaxi" RIGHT
@dump:xp-before-tabaxi

RIGHT
@while "You see here" ESC
e
@choose "(ground) tabaxi corpse"
@while "Stop eating" n
@dump:xp-after-tabaxi
v
@dump:log-tabaxi
@while "Messages" ESC

@quit
